From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kreijstal <elektrischrainbow@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 12:37:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] phi: ThreadDataRegistry must not use std::shared_mutex on
 MinGW

libstdc++'s std::shared_mutex is a winpthreads rwlock initialized with
PTHREAD_RWLOCK_INITIALIZER. winpthreads initializes such a lock lazily on
first use, and that initialization is not thread-safe: a thread racing the
first locker gets EINVAL from pthread_rwlock_wrlock() (rwl_ref() sees the
lock either NULL-ed by pthread_rwlock_init() or already initialized by
rwlock_static_init()). std::shared_mutex::lock() only checks for EDEADLK,
so the caller continues without holding the lock.

The memory-stat registries (paddle::memory::Stat) are first used
concurrently by the PIR interpreter's worker threads. The losing thread
then inserts into tid_map_ while another thread holds the lock, and its
unlock of a lock it never acquired corrupts the rwlock counters. When a
worker thread exits later, ~ThreadDataHolder() (a noexcept TLS destructor)
fails tid_map_.at(tid) with std::out_of_range and the process aborts
("terminate called after throwing ... unordered_map::at"), intermittently
and mostly under CPU load.

Use std::mutex on MinGW, as on macOS. winpthreads initializes static
mutexes with a compare-and-swap, and the registry's shared (reader) paths
are rare.
---
 paddle/phi/common/thread_data_registry.h | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/paddle/phi/common/thread_data_registry.h b/paddle/phi/common/thread_data_registry.h
index ba0fea5b..867df6e2 100644
--- a/paddle/phi/common/thread_data_registry.h
+++ b/paddle/phi/common/thread_data_registry.h
@@ -83,7 +83,18 @@ class ThreadDataRegistry {
 // types
 // Lock types
 #if defined(__clang__) || defined(__GNUC__)  // CLANG or GCC
-#ifndef __APPLE__
+#if defined(__MINGW32__)
+  // MinGW-w64: libstdc++'s std::shared_mutex is a winpthreads rwlock with
+  // PTHREAD_RWLOCK_INITIALIZER, and winpthreads initializes it lazily without
+  // proper synchronization: a thread that races the first locker gets EINVAL
+  // from pthread_rwlock_wrlock(), which std::shared_mutex::lock() ignores. The
+  // registry map is then modified without the lock and the stray unlock
+  // corrupts the rwlock, so a thread exiting later fails tid_map_.at() in
+  // ~ThreadDataHolder() -> std::terminate. std::mutex (CAS-initialized) is
+  // safe; the shared readers here are rare.
+  using LockType = std::mutex;
+  using SharedLockGuardType = std::lock_guard<std::mutex>;
+#elif !defined(__APPLE__)
   using LockType = std::shared_mutex;
   using SharedLockGuardType = std::shared_lock<std::shared_mutex>;
 // Special case : mac. https://github.com/facebook/react-native/issues/31250
